WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:05.480
I'll be in London one week.

00:05.560 --> 00:07.040
At this address.

00:07.120 --> 00:09.360
Finish your business here and join me.

00:12.280 --> 00:13.640
[Thomas] Dear Grace...

00:15.800 --> 00:17.480
Before the war,

00:17.560 --> 00:20.120
when I had an important decision to make,

00:20.200 --> 00:22.200
I used to flip a coin.

00:22.920 --> 00:25.040
Perhaps that is what I will
do again.

00:26.880 --> 00:28.880
[slow song playing]

01:06.880 --> 01:07.920
[gunshot]

01:14.480 --> 01:17.440
[guitar strumming]

01:39.640 --> 01:42.120
[theme song playing]

02:48.520 --> 02:50.160
[priest] ...give him peace.

02:50.240 --> 02:51.520
Amen.

02:59.880 --> 03:01.120
[clears his throat]

03:03.800 --> 03:06.000
I promised my friend,
Freddy Thorne,

03:07.400 --> 03:09.720
that I'd say a few words
over his grave

03:09.800 --> 03:11.640
if he should pass before me.

03:13.480 --> 03:15.280
I made this promise

03:15.360 --> 03:18.120
before he became
me brother-in-law,

03:18.200 --> 03:20.240
when we were in France,

03:20.320 --> 03:21.800
fighting for the King.

03:21.880 --> 03:22.920
Amen.

03:25.040 --> 03:27.840
And in the end,
it wasn't war that took Freddy.

03:27.920 --> 03:29.160
[baby cries]

03:29.240 --> 03:31.160
Pestilence took him.

03:31.240 --> 03:33.320
Come here. Sh-sh-sh.

03:33.400 --> 03:36.400
But Freddy passed on
his soul and his spirit

03:36.480 --> 03:38.200
to a new generation

03:38.280 --> 03:40.120
before he was cruelly taken.

03:41.120 --> 03:43.400
[baby crying]

03:43.880 --> 03:45.960
[people speaking indistinctly]

03:49.040 --> 03:51.200
We thought now Freddy's gone,

03:51.280 --> 03:53.280
you might come back
to Birmingham.

03:56.240 --> 04:00.520
God, do you know how funny it is
that you've got chauffeurs in uniform?

04:00.600 --> 04:02.640
Just for the occasion, Ada.

04:02.720 --> 04:05.840
Do you know how unfair it is
you've got four Bugattis

04:05.920 --> 04:07.880
when half the country's starving?

04:09.480 --> 04:11.640
So now they've made you
ashamed of us?

04:11.720 --> 04:13.280
[clears his throat]

04:13.360 --> 04:16.640
When I think how I used to be,
it makes me embarrassed.

04:16.720 --> 04:18.640
[Polly] Karl's with his cousins.

04:18.720 --> 04:21.600
I caught them trying to pinch
flowers off a grave.

04:23.600 --> 04:24.680
Ada.

04:25.720 --> 04:27.200
Are you coming home?

04:27.280 --> 04:28.560
I'm going home.

04:29.560 --> 04:32.280
It's all right, Pol.
We make Ada embarrassed.

04:32.360 --> 04:33.880
That's not what I said.

04:36.480 --> 04:39.080
There's another reason
we want you home.

04:39.160 --> 04:40.920
We're planning an expansion.

04:41.000 --> 04:42.960
I'm taking premises in London.

04:43.040 --> 04:45.000
It's a funeral,
business can wait.

04:45.080 --> 04:46.200
Polly,

04:46.280 --> 04:48.520
if Ada was weeping
then I'd stop.

04:48.600 --> 04:50.000
But she's not.

04:50.080 --> 04:54.640
The expansion means it'll be dangerous
to be a Shelby in London for a while.

04:54.720 --> 04:56.000
Yeah.

04:56.080 --> 04:58.000
Well, I'm not a Shelby anymore.

05:00.360 --> 05:02.360
And I'm not a Thorne now either.

05:04.320 --> 05:05.640
I'm free.

05:09.120 --> 05:10.800
I have got to get Karl home.

05:18.080 --> 05:21.880
-[exhales]
-I told you, let me do it.

05:21.960 --> 05:23.400
It's all right.

05:23.480 --> 05:26.880
I'll have some men watch her house
till the danger passes.

05:32.800 --> 05:34.960
[Polly] "Till the danger passes."

05:37.600 --> 05:39.320
That'll be the bloody day.

05:39.600 --> 05:41.760
[dramatic music playing]

05:49.000 --> 05:50.960
[people speaking indistinctly]

06:15.680 --> 06:18.640
[Moss] It happened at exactly 7:00 a.m.

06:18.720 --> 06:22.240
Nobody saw anything.
Our patrols were not in the area.

06:25.080 --> 06:28.360
Mr. Shelby, do you have any idea
who might have done it?

06:30.160 --> 06:32.720
I'd say it was something to do
with the gas.

06:32.800 --> 06:33.960
[clears his throat]

06:35.280 --> 06:36.840
It's just been fitted.

06:39.880 --> 06:42.560
Madam, the structure is
not yet declared safe.

06:43.160 --> 06:44.160
[debris crunches]

06:44.240 --> 06:45.360
Madam!

06:55.920 --> 06:57.560
This is all over the place.

07:02.640 --> 07:03.920
Confetti.

07:08.400 --> 07:09.840
You can go.

07:09.920 --> 07:11.160
Right.

07:17.080 --> 07:18.200
Who?

07:21.840 --> 07:23.520
Who did this to us?

07:42.000 --> 07:44.200
Whiskey, Irish.

07:44.520 --> 07:45.760
[clears his throat]

07:58.720 --> 08:02.040
I heard there was a bit of a bang
in your part of town.

08:02.120 --> 08:04.400
Gas and electric don't mix.

08:04.480 --> 08:06.560
Who would have thought
they would?

08:06.640 --> 08:08.760
How's business otherwise,
Mr. Shelby?

08:11.200 --> 08:13.320
You know something?

08:13.400 --> 08:17.560
In these times of hunger and hardship,
business is surprisingly good.

08:20.960 --> 08:23.880
[boy] Which one of you
is the Peaky Blinder devil?

08:25.320 --> 08:29.720
[rhythmic music playing]

08:30.960 --> 08:32.120
Gentlemen.

08:33.240 --> 08:36.080
[tense music]

08:56.040 --> 08:59.040
[music continues]

09:26.240 --> 09:28.520
[indistinct chatter]

09:36.560 --> 09:39.120
[music continues]

09:41.040 --> 09:42.680
[John] Finn! Get in here.

09:42.760 --> 09:44.680
Finn, hold the phone, come here.

09:46.200 --> 09:47.800
-Take this.
-Yeah, sure.

09:52.160 --> 09:54.520
[music continues]

09:59.600 --> 10:01.320
[John] Polly.

10:01.600 --> 10:03.440
Did he say who did it?

10:03.520 --> 10:05.440
He's gone to the Black Lion.

10:06.240 --> 10:07.760
[John] On his own?

10:07.840 --> 10:10.320
Tommy does everything
on his own.

10:10.640 --> 10:11.920
Should I go to there?

10:12.120 --> 10:13.320
What?

10:13.400 --> 10:14.600
Should I go see him?

10:14.680 --> 10:15.680
No!

10:16.480 --> 10:18.080
Where the fuck is Arthur?

10:18.160 --> 10:21.520
Protecting the Garrison's whiskey
from the police.

10:21.800 --> 10:23.120
For fuck sake!

10:27.680 --> 10:29.120
Polly.

10:30.040 --> 10:32.880
Feels like things are getting out of hand.

10:34.000 --> 10:35.560
So get 'em in hand.

10:37.320 --> 10:39.640
Know what Ada said to me this morning?

10:42.200 --> 10:45.520
She said we all look like
we work in a factory

10:45.600 --> 10:47.200
under the ground.

10:48.360 --> 10:50.120
She said we look like ghosts.

10:51.560 --> 10:52.680
She'll be back.

10:53.760 --> 10:55.000
When?

10:55.360 --> 10:56.840
When she needs us.

10:58.240 --> 10:59.960
And anyway,

11:00.160 --> 11:02.720
who the fuck would
blow up our pub?

11:02.920 --> 11:04.000
Six.

11:04.080 --> 11:05.360
Six what?

11:05.440 --> 11:08.280
Six questions since
you walked through that door.

11:10.880 --> 11:14.160
Soon you'll have to start being the man
with the answers.

11:16.360 --> 11:17.560
Why?

11:19.360 --> 11:20.520
Seven.

11:22.320 --> 11:25.400
When London happens,
you'll have to hold up your end.

11:26.520 --> 11:28.680
Or we'll find somebody else who can.

11:41.760 --> 11:42.760
[door opens]

11:44.040 --> 11:45.200
[door closes]

11:45.280 --> 11:47.040
Thomas Shelby.

11:48.840 --> 11:50.400
You blew up my pub.

11:51.720 --> 11:54.040
Anger defeats fear. Good.

11:55.160 --> 11:56.840
You blew up my pub.

11:58.880 --> 12:01.360
Tommy has a reputation
to uphold.

12:01.600 --> 12:04.640
A reputation for not being
scared of anything.

12:04.720 --> 12:08.880
In all the world, violent men
are the easiest to deal with.

12:11.920 --> 12:15.600
So tell me, which brand of rebel
are you, eh?

12:16.000 --> 12:20.400
I read somewhere that you paddies started
fighting amongst yourselves now.

12:21.520 --> 12:25.720
The King offers you a peace treaty
and you start a war about it.

12:25.800 --> 12:27.440
That's funny,

12:27.520 --> 12:29.040
don't you think?

12:29.120 --> 12:30.760
A war about peace.

12:35.240 --> 12:38.080
So are you for the treaty
or against the treaty?

12:38.160 --> 12:40.000
Forgive me, I get confused.

12:47.520 --> 12:50.800
You are one decision away from death,
Mr. Shelby.

12:51.720 --> 12:53.560
So stop fucking smiling.

12:57.200 --> 12:59.080
Your name is Irene O'Donell.

12:59.160 --> 13:02.720
You have a son at the
Cherry Wood Road School in Harborne.

13:02.800 --> 13:05.880
He has irons on his legs.
His name is Sean.

13:05.960 --> 13:08.680
He comes last in every race,

13:08.760 --> 13:09.880
poor boy.

13:11.200 --> 13:13.720
Poor boy if the race was important.

13:14.080 --> 13:16.840
Do you know what I mean,
Irene O'Donell?

13:16.920 --> 13:19.680
There are other ways
to carry out this mission.

13:19.760 --> 13:22.800
Allow me to put a bullet
in this scum tinker's head.

13:29.680 --> 13:30.840
[softly] No.

13:32.080 --> 13:34.800
He researches his enemies.

13:34.880 --> 13:36.680
That's why he's been chosen.

13:38.240 --> 13:39.880
I am chosen?

13:42.040 --> 13:43.160
I am chosen.

13:45.600 --> 13:47.080
Can the chosen one smoke?

14:01.960 --> 14:04.360
A vacancy has appeared

14:04.440 --> 14:06.400
and you are going to fill it.

14:09.280 --> 14:10.560
Chosen by who?

14:12.400 --> 14:14.120
By an informed consensus.

14:16.160 --> 14:17.880
I have things to do.

14:19.480 --> 14:22.160
So perhaps you could tell
the chosen one

14:22.240 --> 14:24.520
what he has been chosen for.

14:25.800 --> 14:28.000
From now on, Mr. Shelby,

14:28.080 --> 14:32.640
you shut your fucking Gypsy mouth
and listen to your instructions.

14:35.520 --> 14:38.160
[eerie violin music playing]

14:46.600 --> 14:48.680
[creaking]

14:59.000 --> 15:00.840
[tense music amplifies]

15:00.920 --> 15:02.440
[shouts] Fuck!

15:04.120 --> 15:06.280
[music continues]

15:16.360 --> 15:18.640
[breathes deeply]

15:32.280 --> 15:34.840
[eerie music continues]

15:39.240 --> 15:41.800
[heavy beat music playing]

16:02.680 --> 16:05.480
[suspenseful music playing]

16:12.720 --> 16:14.840
[clanging metal sounds]

16:14.920 --> 16:17.720
I need the area around
the co-operative stables

16:17.800 --> 16:21.080
clear of coppers between
midnight and 4:00 tonight.

16:21.320 --> 16:23.160
Right. Can I ask why?

16:25.840 --> 16:29.560
Well, whatever it is you're going to do,
don't start any fires.

16:29.640 --> 16:32.920
The firemen go out
on strike at midnight.

16:33.800 --> 16:35.760
That's all. You can go.

16:41.200 --> 16:44.320
I've got some information
you might be interested in.

16:45.000 --> 16:46.240
No charge.

16:47.680 --> 16:51.680
An old friend of ours
coming back to the city.

16:51.760 --> 16:53.600
Just passing through, he says.

16:54.320 --> 16:55.920
He's very grand these days.

16:56.000 --> 16:58.720
He's head of some secret department.

16:58.800 --> 17:00.280
The Irish desk.

17:01.600 --> 17:03.120
So,

17:03.400 --> 17:06.160
I doubt he'll be bothering
the likes of us, eh?

17:07.800 --> 17:09.320
I'll say goodnight, Tom.

17:15.680 --> 17:17.840
[metal clicking]

17:17.920 --> 17:22.640
[condemned man] I demand to speak to
a representative of His Majesty the King!

17:22.720 --> 17:25.680
A representative of His Majesty the King!

17:25.760 --> 17:29.920
[condemned man continues shouting]

17:30.080 --> 17:32.200
Father! Father!

17:32.280 --> 17:35.240
It was the Ulsterman,
the man with the wolf cane. 

17:35.320 --> 17:36.840
Get the fuck off me.

17:36.920 --> 17:39.680
You must reconcile with
the fate that awaits.

17:39.760 --> 17:43.360
I demand to speak to a representative of
His Majesty the King!

17:43.440 --> 17:44.960
Make your peace with God.

17:45.040 --> 17:47.680
A representative of His Majesty the King!

17:47.760 --> 17:50.400
-Father. Come on.
-[condemned man] No, wait!

17:50.480 --> 17:51.760
Where are you going?

17:53.680 --> 17:57.040
[Campbell] Does someone here have
business with the King?

17:57.120 --> 17:58.440
Major Campbell?

17:59.280 --> 18:00.600
About time.

18:00.680 --> 18:02.320
[condemned man shouting]

18:02.400 --> 18:05.560
I am a very busy man, Governor.

18:05.640 --> 18:07.240
In seven and a half minutes

18:07.320 --> 18:10.440
the man in that cell is due to be
hanged for murder.

18:10.800 --> 18:13.520
The murder of an Irish activist
in Whitechapel.

18:13.600 --> 18:16.320
Does the case ring any bells,
Major Campbell?

18:17.640 --> 18:19.320
No, Governor.

18:19.400 --> 18:20.840
I hear no bells.

18:20.920 --> 18:22.920
[condemned man
continues shouting]

18:23.000 --> 18:25.640
Since yesterday morning,
he claims the murder

18:25.720 --> 18:29.080
was ordered by the
British Secret Intelligence Service.

18:29.160 --> 18:32.880
It's a little late to be coming up
with nonsense like that.

18:32.960 --> 18:37.280
He was told if he kept his mouth shut,
there'd be a last minute pardon.

18:37.360 --> 18:39.320
Which has not materialized.

18:40.520 --> 18:41.880
Forgive me, Governor,

18:43.000 --> 18:45.360
but what business is this of mine?

18:47.200 --> 18:49.160
He says the man who hired him was

18:49.240 --> 18:51.560
an Intelligence Service Chief of Staff.

18:52.320 --> 18:54.160
An Ulsterman.

18:54.240 --> 18:56.640
Carries a cane with a wolf head handle.

18:56.720 --> 18:58.880
A bullet wound in his right leg.

18:59.560 --> 19:00.760
Nope.

19:02.760 --> 19:04.920
Still no bells. Governor...

19:06.640 --> 19:09.920
you have a very clear remit.

19:10.000 --> 19:14.200
A part of that remit is
to oversee the smooth completion

19:14.280 --> 19:17.760
of executions sanctioned by the Crown.

19:17.840 --> 19:20.600
And in the next four minutes
it is your duty

19:20.680 --> 19:23.160
to escort that man

19:23.240 --> 19:24.640
to the gates of hell.

19:25.400 --> 19:26.920
Is that clear?

19:27.000 --> 19:29.560
[condemned man continues shouting]

19:29.640 --> 19:31.200
And

19:31.280 --> 19:35.480
if you ever have the desire to
discuss this with anyone else...

19:37.360 --> 19:39.760
I know where you live.

19:42.200 --> 19:45.160
[metal sounding cane]

19:54.160 --> 19:55.840
[Finn] Arthur.

19:55.920 --> 19:57.840
Tommy's called a family meeting.

19:59.200 --> 20:00.320
Arthur!

20:00.400 --> 20:02.960
He just beat the
shit out of an apprentice.

20:03.040 --> 20:05.520
I had to take half the kid away
in buckets.

20:13.240 --> 20:14.480
Arthur.

20:19.440 --> 20:21.200
[out of breath] Two thousand.

20:35.760 --> 20:38.000
[breathes in deeply]

20:41.160 --> 20:42.480
Come on.

20:46.800 --> 20:49.400
[fast pace footsteps]

20:51.360 --> 20:52.640
Sit down, Finn.

21:04.920 --> 21:06.760
Where the bloody hell is Tommy?

21:06.840 --> 21:08.160
He's on his way.

21:09.880 --> 21:12.440
While we're waiting patiently...

21:14.600 --> 21:15.920
Whiskey.

21:16.000 --> 21:17.520
[exhales]

21:17.840 --> 21:19.600
Left over from the explosion.

21:21.560 --> 21:23.320
It is good stuff as well.

21:37.720 --> 21:39.160
Right.

21:39.600 --> 21:41.840
Before Tommy gets here,

21:42.280 --> 21:45.720
There's a few things we need to
get straight between us.

21:45.800 --> 21:46.960
You think?

21:48.200 --> 21:49.200
Yeah.

21:50.480 --> 21:52.040
Yeah, I do. I want to know

21:54.080 --> 21:57.280
when did we all take a vote
on this expansion south?

22:01.520 --> 22:04.000
You have anything to say,

22:04.080 --> 22:05.280
you wait for Thomas.

22:05.360 --> 22:06.720
Polly is fucking right.

22:06.800 --> 22:08.240
I see all the books.

22:09.800 --> 22:12.360
Legal and off-track.

22:13.760 --> 22:15.360
Saw stuff you don't see.

22:17.400 --> 22:19.840
In the past year,

22:19.960 --> 22:25.720
the Shelby Company Limited
has been making 150 pounds

22:26.840 --> 22:28.120
a day.

22:30.000 --> 22:31.840
[shouts] Right? A fucking day.

22:31.920 --> 22:33.840
Sometimes more.

22:33.920 --> 22:35.680
So what I want to know

22:37.200 --> 22:40.520
is why are we changing things?

22:40.600 --> 22:43.000
Polly, look what's happened already.

22:43.800 --> 22:46.040
We haven't even set foot
in London yet,

22:46.120 --> 22:48.080
they've already blown up our pub.

22:48.160 --> 22:51.160
-Who said anything about the Cockneys?
-Who else?

22:51.240 --> 22:52.480
You know who did it?

22:52.560 --> 22:54.520
No, she doesn't know who did it.

22:56.400 --> 22:58.840
I'm told only family are
allowed to speak.

22:59.320 --> 23:01.120
Everybody's allowed to speak.

23:01.920 --> 23:03.720
On your feet, Esme.

23:03.800 --> 23:05.720
Let's hear what you have to say.

23:06.320 --> 23:07.520
[John clears throat]

23:08.360 --> 23:09.920
I speak for our household.

23:11.160 --> 23:12.880
-So, could...
-John.

23:12.960 --> 23:17.440
This company is a modern enterprise
and believes in equal rights for women.

23:17.520 --> 23:18.840
On your feet, Esme.

23:31.560 --> 23:34.560
I am not a blood member
of this family.

23:34.920 --> 23:37.120
But perhaps because I am not a member

23:37.200 --> 23:39.480
I can see things in a different light.

23:40.720 --> 23:42.480
So I will get to my point.

23:42.560 --> 23:44.200
That would be nice.

23:44.280 --> 23:48.280
As my husband said, Shelby Company Limited
is now very successful.

23:49.000 --> 23:50.240
But London...

23:51.400 --> 23:54.840
I have kin in Shepherds Bush
and Portobello.

23:54.920 --> 23:58.120
It's more like wars
between armies down there.

23:58.200 --> 24:01.600
And the coppers fight
side by side with them.

24:01.680 --> 24:04.640
And there are foreigners
of every description.

24:06.080 --> 24:08.480
And the use of bombs
is the least of it.

24:11.000 --> 24:12.320
I have a child.

24:13.920 --> 24:16.400
Blessed with the
Shelby family good looks.

24:17.760 --> 24:20.120
I want John to see him grow up.

24:21.360 --> 24:25.360
[Esme] I want us to someday live
somewhere with fresh air and trees.

24:25.440 --> 24:27.400
And keep chickens or something.

24:28.600 --> 24:32.400
But London is just smoke
and trouble, Thomas.

24:32.880 --> 24:34.080
"Thomas"?

24:37.840 --> 24:39.480
That's all I have to say.

24:41.240 --> 24:43.240
[Arthur] That was a lot of words.

24:44.320 --> 24:45.480
A lot of words.

24:48.360 --> 24:50.320
Wash them down with a nice drink.

24:51.280 --> 24:52.600
Thank you, Esme.

24:54.880 --> 24:58.280
Firstly, the bang in the pub
had nothing to do with London.

24:58.360 --> 24:59.320
Understood?

24:59.400 --> 25:02.920
The bang is something I'm dealing with
on my own. Secondly,

25:03.000 --> 25:06.040
we have nothing to fear
from the business expansion

25:06.120 --> 25:07.840
so long as we stick together.

25:08.640 --> 25:13.200
After the first few weeks, nine-tenths
of what we do in London will be legal.

25:13.280 --> 25:16.840
The other tenth is in good hands.
Isn't that right Arthur?

25:16.920 --> 25:18.080
That's right.

25:19.320 --> 25:22.040
Some of you have expressed
your reservations.

25:22.120 --> 25:23.520
Fair enough.

25:23.600 --> 25:27.040
Any of you who want no part in
the future of this company,

25:27.120 --> 25:28.640
walk out the door.

25:32.880 --> 25:34.120
Right now.

25:35.640 --> 25:37.280
Go and raise your chickens.

25:39.880 --> 25:41.800
But those of you with ambition?

25:42.640 --> 25:44.880
The expansion process begins tomorrow.

25:58.480 --> 26:00.000
Tomorrow?

26:00.440 --> 26:03.440
I'm company treasurer.
You should speak to me first.

26:04.960 --> 26:06.720
It's Newmarket tomorrow.

26:06.800 --> 26:08.640
Third busiest day of the year.

26:08.720 --> 26:09.840
We have 18 staff.

26:09.920 --> 26:12.200
Who you trust with 200-quid takings?

26:12.280 --> 26:14.440
And I changed the combination.

26:17.560 --> 26:18.560
[Tommy exhales]

26:18.640 --> 26:21.400
So, what's going on, Thomas?

26:22.600 --> 26:24.720
Who did you meet
at the Black Lion?

26:26.560 --> 26:28.480
Give me the combination, Polly.

26:34.800 --> 26:36.440
Polly.

26:36.520 --> 26:38.280
Give me the combination.

26:44.400 --> 26:47.200
What happened to the pub
is Irish business.

26:47.280 --> 26:49.720
In this situation, for everyone's safety

26:49.800 --> 26:52.520
it's best if some things
remain undisclosed.

26:53.440 --> 26:54.640
So why tomorrow?

26:57.480 --> 26:59.640
Like you said,
tomorrow is Newmarket.

26:59.720 --> 27:02.280
All the London bosses
will be at the races.

27:02.360 --> 27:04.680
So you just roll up and take the city?

27:04.760 --> 27:06.040
No.

27:06.120 --> 27:08.600
We take the opportunity to
show our hand.

27:10.480 --> 27:12.720
The Italian gangs and the Jewish gangs

27:12.800 --> 27:15.200
have been at war in London for 6 months.

27:15.280 --> 27:18.320
-It's not our war.
-The Jews need allies.

27:18.400 --> 27:19.680
Yeah, but we don't.

27:19.760 --> 27:20.960
We need a foothold.

27:21.040 --> 27:23.360
At the Southern end of the Grand Union.

27:23.440 --> 27:25.440
The Jews control Camden Town.

27:29.200 --> 27:31.240
[Tommy exhales deeply]

27:33.480 --> 27:35.440
Your mother said,

27:35.520 --> 27:37.840
"It's his cleverness that'll kill him."

27:39.080 --> 27:41.400
No one gets killed, Polly.

27:41.480 --> 27:45.080
We go down tomorrow when it's
quiet and we leave our message.

27:45.160 --> 27:48.080
If Alfie Solomons and his Camden boys
come to us,

27:48.160 --> 27:51.320
we'll negotiate the use of a
secure bonded warehouse.

27:51.400 --> 27:53.840
Our legal activities in London can begin.

27:53.920 --> 27:57.520
Now, please,
open the fucking safe.

28:04.200 --> 28:08.200
Do you know it was a fine speech
you made in there,

28:08.360 --> 28:11.880
about this company believing
in equal rights for women.

28:12.960 --> 28:14.840
But when it comes to it,

28:14.920 --> 28:17.120
you don't listen to a word we say.

28:18.320 --> 28:19.880
Maybe you don't trust us.

28:22.920 --> 28:24.640
She was one woman, Thomas.

28:31.520 --> 28:33.760
Maybe it's time you forgot
about her.

28:33.840 --> 28:35.600
Forgot about who?

28:35.680 --> 28:36.760
[exhales]

28:38.480 --> 28:40.400
[safe opening]

28:49.560 --> 28:52.280
You and the boys go
and get yourselves killed.

29:07.080 --> 29:08.520
[breathing heavily]

29:43.680 --> 29:45.200
You're going to London?

29:48.080 --> 29:49.560
No.

29:49.880 --> 29:51.880
Something I have to do first.

29:51.960 --> 29:55.160
Huh, I've got a typewriter
like that.

29:55.680 --> 29:57.640
Got it out of a catalogue.

29:57.920 --> 30:00.120
I'm doing a correspondence course.

30:03.120 --> 30:05.480
I'm learning to do it
with my eyes shut.

30:05.560 --> 30:07.240
It's a test you have to do.

30:14.520 --> 30:16.440
Will you come back
before you go?

30:21.320 --> 30:22.400
No.

30:35.880 --> 30:37.920
[Tommy exhales]

30:39.360 --> 30:42.240
I wish just once you wouldn't pay me.

30:42.960 --> 30:45.000
As if we were ordinary people.

30:45.320 --> 30:46.360
Yeah.

30:46.840 --> 30:47.880
[door opens]

30:49.040 --> 30:50.000
[door closes]

31:02.760 --> 31:05.080
[dramatic music playing]

31:09.280 --> 31:11.320
[wind blowing]

31:50.080 --> 31:51.320
Let's begin.

31:52.200 --> 31:53.680
Hands on the table.

32:01.280 --> 32:03.840
Tonight we have two new pilgrims
joining us.

32:03.920 --> 32:05.840
So let's welcome them.

32:11.520 --> 32:12.760
Starting with you.

32:13.560 --> 32:15.960
Who is it that you are seeking to reach?

32:16.680 --> 32:18.440
My husband.

32:18.880 --> 32:21.720
He was taken six months ago
by the influenza.

32:23.000 --> 32:26.080
I tried to reach him through
Mrs. Breach in Sparkhill

32:26.160 --> 32:28.720
but she kept getting
his middle name wrong.

32:28.800 --> 32:31.840
[medium] Don't talk about
Mrs. Breach in this house. 

32:33.000 --> 32:35.800
She is an unsanctified charlatan.

32:40.720 --> 32:41.920
And you?

32:44.320 --> 32:45.760
Who do you seek?

32:47.520 --> 32:48.960
Well...

32:49.360 --> 32:52.480
Sorry, the truth is,
I'm not even sure she's dead.

32:52.880 --> 32:54.520
So I came here to find out.

32:59.640 --> 33:00.680
Uh...

33:02.960 --> 33:05.040
You see, my son and my daughter

33:05.280 --> 33:08.240
were taken from me
when they were very small.

33:08.320 --> 33:11.920
By the Parish authorities.
I never knew what happened to them.

33:13.000 --> 33:14.280
But

33:15.320 --> 33:16.520
lately,

33:18.080 --> 33:19.440
I have had a feeling.

33:21.680 --> 33:23.040
Like a feeling...

33:27.360 --> 33:28.960
I can't put it into words.

33:30.800 --> 33:33.160
And I keep having a dream.

33:33.240 --> 33:34.960
[eerie music playing]

33:36.560 --> 33:39.160
I see a pretty girl,

33:40.560 --> 33:42.160
about 18 years old,

33:43.840 --> 33:46.120
she is standing across the street,

33:48.280 --> 33:51.880
and she tells me she has passed over.

33:52.840 --> 33:56.560
Now my daughter would've been
18 this year.

33:57.560 --> 33:59.840
On May 15th.

34:05.280 --> 34:08.040
And this girl has dark eyes

34:08.120 --> 34:09.480
like mine.

34:13.560 --> 34:15.120
And she shouts

34:16.120 --> 34:17.480
and she shouts.

34:19.080 --> 34:21.040
And she tells me

34:21.440 --> 34:23.440
she wants to talk to me

34:23.800 --> 34:25.720
because I am her mother.

34:25.800 --> 34:27.880
[eerie music continues]

34:30.000 --> 34:33.880
Now, I don't even know
what name they gave her

34:34.520 --> 34:36.440
after they stole her from me.

34:39.600 --> 34:41.720
But if she does want to
say goodbye,

34:43.120 --> 34:45.160
I thought this would be the place.

34:49.040 --> 34:51.080
You're wearing the Black Madonna.

34:51.880 --> 34:53.040
You're Gypsy?

34:54.440 --> 34:56.800
The part of me that dreams is Gypsy.

35:01.560 --> 35:03.680
I am Gypsy too.

35:06.080 --> 35:07.720
May I ask
your name?

35:12.080 --> 35:14.040
My maiden name is Shelby.

35:23.840 --> 35:25.200
So...

35:26.880 --> 35:28.720
Perhaps you could do me first.

35:37.400 --> 35:39.320
[weeping]

35:40.160 --> 35:41.560
[screams]

35:42.440 --> 35:43.600
No!

35:45.400 --> 35:46.680
No!

35:53.720 --> 35:55.080
[whimpering] No.

35:58.760 --> 36:00.120
[weeps] No.

36:02.080 --> 36:03.440
[metal clanging]

36:03.520 --> 36:04.840
[horses neighing]

36:08.120 --> 36:09.760
[clanging continues]

36:40.120 --> 36:42.560
[slow music playing]

37:55.360 --> 37:56.840
Amen, Duggan.

38:20.640 --> 38:22.600
[Arthur] All right.
Tommy's here.

38:22.720 --> 38:24.000
[John] Hang on.

38:30.320 --> 38:31.560
John!

38:31.640 --> 38:32.880
[John] I'm coming! 

38:39.080 --> 38:40.240
[exhales]

38:41.400 --> 38:43.160
7:00,

38:43.240 --> 38:46.280
12:00, 10:00 if I'm still sober.

38:46.520 --> 38:48.000
I got it from the doctor.

38:49.520 --> 38:51.440
Keeps me nice and calm.

38:53.320 --> 38:57.200
Same thing they gave us in the trenches
to stop us fucking wanking.

38:57.280 --> 39:00.680
Polly said it's good for me temper.
It slows me down.

39:00.760 --> 39:02.880
Arthur, there are some things

39:02.960 --> 39:05.680
Polly doesn't understand.
I need you fast.

39:05.760 --> 39:07.720
Not slow, eh?

39:07.800 --> 39:09.840
She wouldn't let go of my leg.

39:09.920 --> 39:12.200
That's not all she wouldn't let go of.

39:12.280 --> 39:14.280
You know she's against this, Tom.

39:14.360 --> 39:15.640
She's got opinions.

39:15.720 --> 39:17.440
Nothing wrong with opinions.

39:17.520 --> 39:19.600
-Get in the fucking car.
-Shut up!

39:21.880 --> 39:24.000
[upbeat music playing]

39:24.080 --> 39:25.320
[shouts] Right!

39:25.600 --> 39:28.520
The Peaky Blinders are going on holiday!

39:28.600 --> 39:30.160
Sit down, you mad bastard.

39:30.240 --> 39:32.480
[all laughing]

39:34.720 --> 39:36.080
[door opens]

39:36.840 --> 39:38.160
[door slams]

39:41.120 --> 39:42.760
[fast pace footsteps]

39:55.680 --> 39:57.840
You're against this too, aren't you?

40:00.280 --> 40:03.320
Look out for anyone putting
big money on Divine Star

40:03.400 --> 40:05.520
at the 3:30 at Newmarket.

40:05.880 --> 40:07.320
She's one of ours.

40:08.480 --> 40:11.280
Anything over a pound, tell me.

40:17.920 --> 40:19.080
Polly?

40:20.280 --> 40:22.600
I don't wish to pry into your business.

40:23.160 --> 40:25.000
But you should know something.

40:26.160 --> 40:27.880
That woman is a trickster.

40:28.520 --> 40:30.320
What woman?

40:30.480 --> 40:33.400
Her sister was in
the wash house early,

40:33.600 --> 40:36.280
boasting there'd been a Shelby
at the table.

40:40.120 --> 40:41.320
What woman?

40:42.200 --> 40:44.000
Gypsies talk to each other.

40:46.080 --> 40:47.800
What woman?

40:50.200 --> 40:53.200
You went to see Mrs. Price
in the Patch last night.

40:58.240 --> 40:59.360
I'm sorry.

41:00.560 --> 41:02.120
So, what do you know?

41:02.200 --> 41:04.240
I know they push the glass.

41:04.360 --> 41:07.960
The man, he's her cousin.
He pushes the glass. It's a trick.

41:08.040 --> 41:10.320
They tell you what you
already believe.

41:12.080 --> 41:15.120
She set up after the war
because of all the widows.

41:15.720 --> 41:16.960
Polly...

41:17.480 --> 41:19.160
I thought you should know.

41:19.240 --> 41:21.160
And in this fucking wash house,

41:21.240 --> 41:23.240
did they tell you
why I went there?

41:29.760 --> 41:31.720
You tell a soul

41:31.800 --> 41:33.840
in this family,

41:33.920 --> 41:37.160
and I swear I will cut you.

41:39.160 --> 41:43.120
I don't need a knife to stop me
telling secrets given in confidence.

41:44.640 --> 41:46.480
It is a matter of honor.

42:06.080 --> 42:07.440
[door slams]

42:07.520 --> 42:09.480
[Arthur] Look at this. Look.

42:09.560 --> 42:11.040
I love it.

42:11.160 --> 42:13.560
Your Esme was right
about one thing.

42:13.640 --> 42:15.800
You can't beat the countryside.

42:17.000 --> 42:18.200
[Arthur] You know,

42:18.280 --> 42:21.120
I think I want to live in
the country one day.

42:21.200 --> 42:23.160
And keep chickens.

42:23.240 --> 42:25.880
-Yeah.
-We will see you in London, Arthur.

42:30.120 --> 42:31.480
For fuck sake!

42:31.560 --> 42:32.680
Take this.

42:35.800 --> 42:37.480
[Tommy] We need to bury him.

42:37.560 --> 42:39.320
[John] Who the fuck is that?

42:39.400 --> 42:43.480
It's Irish business. I thought it best
if I dealt with it on me own.

42:43.840 --> 42:46.280
Come on, we did 1,000 of these in France.

42:46.360 --> 42:48.040
John, grab his head.

42:48.120 --> 42:50.280
So we're not really going to London.

42:50.840 --> 42:52.680
Once we bury him,

42:52.920 --> 42:54.640
then the holiday begins.

42:57.880 --> 43:00.080
[upbeat music playing]

43:01.320 --> 43:03.480
[people laughing]

43:04.440 --> 43:05.760
[horn tooting]

43:10.400 --> 43:12.320
[music continues]

43:20.320 --> 43:21.680
[Arthur] Much obliged.

43:23.920 --> 43:26.880
[swing music playing
in the background]

43:26.960 --> 43:28.520
[indistinct laughter]

43:31.000 --> 43:33.160
It is a fucking freak show.

43:37.200 --> 43:39.280
[music continues in the background]

43:52.720 --> 43:54.920
[Arthur] What the fuck
is that racket?

43:55.000 --> 43:57.800
[Tommy] This is what they call
music these days.

43:57.880 --> 43:58.960
[Arthur] Music?

44:16.360 --> 44:17.400
Oi!

44:17.480 --> 44:18.480
Oi!

44:18.600 --> 44:19.720
Put it away.

44:19.800 --> 44:21.040
[Arthur] Fuck off!

44:26.520 --> 44:28.840
Look at this spot, eh?

44:28.920 --> 44:30.440
It's all right, isn't it?

44:35.120 --> 44:36.920
Irish whiskey. Bottle.

44:38.240 --> 44:39.560
And hurry up!

44:44.040 --> 44:47.280
Fucking hell,
I recognize a few of these lads.

44:49.560 --> 44:52.280
Sabini's cousin over there.

44:52.360 --> 44:55.040
[Tommy] That's right,
this is Sabini's club.

44:55.160 --> 44:56.640
[John] Jesus Christ!

44:56.720 --> 44:58.720
Everybody here is a fucking face.

44:58.800 --> 45:02.560
Just the lieutenants, John.
No sign of the officers.

45:04.880 --> 45:06.440
Let's line them up.

45:08.000 --> 45:09.160
Holiday.

45:11.480 --> 45:15.240
Gentlemen, there's been a mistake.
You're gonna have to leave.

45:16.840 --> 45:18.800
We just bought a fucking bottle.

45:18.880 --> 45:22.280
Some here recognize you
from the racetracks in the north.

45:22.360 --> 45:24.800
Yeah, we get that a lot.

45:24.880 --> 45:29.800
They say you have no business coming
south of the line without prior agreement.

45:29.880 --> 45:32.200
And what line would that be,
my friend?

45:33.120 --> 45:35.400
They say this is provocation.

45:37.400 --> 45:40.480
Right, well, you tell them
we're on holiday.

45:42.040 --> 45:43.680
You're breaking the rules.

45:45.760 --> 45:48.840
They say you are the Peaky Blinders.

45:48.960 --> 45:50.440
Peaky scum!

45:51.320 --> 45:53.560
[band continues playing]

46:13.920 --> 46:15.840
Who the fuck is next?

46:19.200 --> 46:20.360
Come here!

46:26.680 --> 46:28.320
[band stops playing]

46:29.000 --> 46:30.800
Put some ice on 'em!

46:34.960 --> 46:36.280
Get out.

46:39.840 --> 46:40.960
Yeah?

46:43.520 --> 46:44.960
Yeah?

46:45.280 --> 46:46.800
Are you going to use that?

46:47.160 --> 46:48.960
[breathes deeply]

46:51.280 --> 46:52.560
Didn't think so.

47:10.160 --> 47:12.160
We came here

47:12.240 --> 47:13.960
not to make enemies.

47:15.520 --> 47:16.720
No.

47:18.040 --> 47:20.520
We came here to make
new friends.

47:25.080 --> 47:27.440
Those of you who are last

47:27.680 --> 47:29.360
will soon be first.

47:33.560 --> 47:36.320
And those who are downtrodden

47:36.400 --> 47:37.960
will rise up.

47:39.800 --> 47:40.880
Yup.

47:42.360 --> 47:44.040
You know where to find us.

47:52.720 --> 47:54.960
[Arthur] I think I lost a tooth.

47:55.320 --> 47:57.680
I'll have none left at this rate.

47:57.760 --> 47:59.680
Some fucking holiday this is.

47:59.760 --> 48:02.600
You all right without your
medicine now, Arthur?

48:02.680 --> 48:04.840
Here. This'll fix you.

48:04.920 --> 48:07.040
-Give me that.
-You John boy, eh?

48:07.120 --> 48:09.400
How are you?
Or should I ask your wife?

48:09.480 --> 48:10.360
Give over!

48:10.440 --> 48:12.480
No more talk of chickens, hear me?

48:12.560 --> 48:13.680
Fuck the chickens.

48:13.800 --> 48:15.440
I got 50 quid in my pocket.

48:15.520 --> 48:17.040
Let's paint the town, eh?

48:17.120 --> 48:19.160
[imitates chicken sound]

48:19.240 --> 48:20.280
[knock at door]

48:20.360 --> 48:21.680
[Churchill] Come.

48:21.760 --> 48:23.080
[metal sounding cane]

48:24.800 --> 48:27.120
-Oh, dear God.
-[Churchill] Good Lord!

48:27.200 --> 48:29.400
I assumed it was Betty with tea.

48:30.120 --> 48:31.600
Forgive me, sir.

48:31.680 --> 48:33.760
Your secretary was not at the desk.

48:33.840 --> 48:37.120
No. Because she takes lunch
like normal people.

48:37.520 --> 48:41.200
You can turn around, this lady
is a professional life-model.

48:41.400 --> 48:43.040
She does this for a living.

48:44.880 --> 48:47.120
I'm happy to come back later, sir.

48:47.200 --> 48:50.120
No, no, no. Later, I'm in the house.
Just, um...

48:52.040 --> 48:54.480
Keep your back turned
if you must.

48:54.560 --> 48:55.720
[mumbles] Yes, sir.

48:59.320 --> 49:03.800
I would guess you haven't been exposed
to Bohemian society, Major?

49:04.920 --> 49:08.240
Well, I--I--I play cards on occasion.

49:08.320 --> 49:12.880
You are a stranger to cocaine
and exotic dance, too, I imagine.

49:12.960 --> 49:17.120
I--I find the more obvious
vices the easiest to resist.

49:17.200 --> 49:22.640
It's the sneaky little obscure ones
that bring you to your knees, yes?

49:22.720 --> 49:26.760
So, what business is so urgent
it trumps lunch?

49:26.840 --> 49:31.480
Well, sir, our man in Birmingham has
passed his first test with flying colors.

49:31.560 --> 49:33.200
I am horrible with faces.

49:33.280 --> 49:36.320
So I think we can begin to
prepare him for...

49:37.400 --> 49:38.800
for the bigger task.

49:38.880 --> 49:41.200
Expressions elude me.
I think too much.

49:41.280 --> 49:42.680
Sir,

49:42.760 --> 49:45.560
I need your authority

49:45.640 --> 49:48.880
to proceed with all urgency

49:48.960 --> 49:52.440
in preparing the new man
for the bigger task.

49:52.520 --> 49:55.160
You're talking about your bookmaker.

49:55.240 --> 49:56.600
Yes, sir.

49:56.680 --> 50:00.040
You have a history with this man.
Why did you choose him?

50:01.280 --> 50:05.120
To apply pressure on a man
for this type of work

50:05.200 --> 50:10.520
you have to know his weaknesses.
I know this man's weaknesses intimately.

50:10.600 --> 50:12.920
And after mission accomplished,

50:13.000 --> 50:15.960
do we trust him to keep
his mouth shut?

50:16.040 --> 50:17.560
Absolutely not, sir.

50:19.120 --> 50:21.480
So when he has served
his purpose,

50:21.560 --> 50:23.320
he'll be consigned to history

50:23.400 --> 50:25.960
in exactly the same way
as his predecessor.

50:26.040 --> 50:28.160
You mean at the end of a rope.

50:29.600 --> 50:32.560
Well, the end of a rope
has been this man's destiny

50:33.520 --> 50:35.240
since the night he was born.

50:41.600 --> 50:44.320
[dramatic music playing]

51:02.000 --> 51:03.080
[door opens]

51:04.520 --> 51:05.720
You're early.

51:07.280 --> 51:08.720
Shut your eyes, Lizzie.

51:09.840 --> 51:10.840
What?

51:10.920 --> 51:12.280
Shut your eyes.

51:12.360 --> 51:13.840
Go on, shut your eyes.

51:15.760 --> 51:17.800
Good. Now, come with me.

51:18.120 --> 51:19.480
No, shut your eyes.

51:19.560 --> 51:21.480
-[she chuckles]
-This way.

51:21.560 --> 51:23.120
What are you doing, Tom?

51:23.200 --> 51:25.240
You're gonna sit down here, right.

51:26.520 --> 51:27.960
Sit down.

51:28.480 --> 51:30.440
Now... Okay.

51:31.080 --> 51:32.840
-Type this.
-Why?

51:32.920 --> 51:34.760
Lizzie, type this.

51:34.840 --> 51:37.920
If... Put your cigarette out.

51:38.000 --> 51:40.040
Right. If winter comes,

51:42.880 --> 51:44.680
then can spring be far behind?

51:45.040 --> 51:46.400
Why?

51:46.640 --> 51:48.120
Did you say, "Spring"?

51:48.200 --> 51:50.160
Lizzie, what comes after winter?

51:53.800 --> 51:55.520
Let's see.

51:55.600 --> 51:56.680
It's a question.

51:56.760 --> 51:58.760
Put in a question mark.

52:00.040 --> 52:01.520
Now, type this.

52:01.840 --> 52:03.080
Wanted.

52:03.160 --> 52:05.440
Secretary for an expanding business...

52:05.520 --> 52:06.640
Slow down.

52:06.720 --> 52:09.520
Must be able to take dictation
and touch-type.

52:10.360 --> 52:11.800
Five days a week.

52:11.880 --> 52:13.480
Um...

52:13.560 --> 52:15.920
Eight pounds and four shillings
a month.

52:16.000 --> 52:18.040
Must be able to start immediately.

52:19.880 --> 52:21.040
Are you serious?

52:24.440 --> 52:26.680
Things are starting to happen, Lizzie.

52:28.880 --> 52:31.880
I need someone who can look
the other way sometimes.

52:39.120 --> 52:41.520
You can stop the other work too, Lizzie.

52:42.640 --> 52:45.040
All of it, this time.

52:47.000 --> 52:48.320
No exceptions.

52:54.520 --> 52:57.480
8:00, Monday morning,
the upstairs office.

52:57.560 --> 52:58.800
Don't be late.

53:05.760 --> 53:07.800
[footsteps]

53:08.880 --> 53:09.880
[door opens]

53:11.640 --> 53:13.360
Newmarket was profitable.

53:14.080 --> 53:16.560
Arthur told me how you left
your message.

53:17.600 --> 53:20.120
When I asked him
where his medicine was,

53:20.200 --> 53:21.920
he said you poured it away.

53:22.000 --> 53:24.760
All I poured away was
opium and bromide.

53:24.840 --> 53:26.920
That's how it works in London?

53:27.000 --> 53:29.600
Every boss has to have a mad dog
at his side.

53:29.680 --> 53:32.920
Somebody who can't be predicted,
mad in the head.

53:33.000 --> 53:35.360
But Thomas Shelby uses
his own brother.

53:35.440 --> 53:37.040
Stop fucking fighting me!

53:37.120 --> 53:38.440
Somebody has to!

53:39.560 --> 53:41.120
That arrived an hour ago.

53:41.200 --> 53:44.640
There is no name on it
but it came from Camden Town.

53:44.720 --> 53:47.520
I was going to burn it. I should have.

53:47.600 --> 53:49.160
Well done, Tommy.

53:49.240 --> 53:52.880
You picked a side.
Now you're at war with Sabini.

54:32.760 --> 54:35.200
Ada? Are you coming for a drink?

54:35.280 --> 54:38.000
No. The woman upstairs
is looking after Karl.

54:38.080 --> 54:39.880
She goes mad if I'm late.

54:57.800 --> 54:59.920
[footsteps]

55:11.240 --> 55:13.160
-Ada Shelby?
-No.

55:14.520 --> 55:17.120
[grunting]

55:21.840 --> 55:23.640
Your brother broke the rules.

55:23.720 --> 55:25.120
I don't have a brother.

55:25.200 --> 55:27.760
Me and my friends need female company.

55:27.840 --> 55:29.200
Let's go for a drive.

55:30.240 --> 55:31.360
[Ada] Get off!

55:42.960 --> 55:45.040
[Sabini gangster]
Fucking hold him.

55:48.240 --> 55:50.200
[grunts]

56:14.240 --> 56:15.760
[Sabini] Tommy Shelby.

56:16.520 --> 56:18.720
I missed you in my club.

56:18.800 --> 56:20.400
I was at the races.

56:21.320 --> 56:22.640
Sabini.

56:23.400 --> 56:24.880
Uh...

56:24.960 --> 56:26.440
Don't say my name.

56:27.560 --> 56:29.320
Jesus.

56:29.400 --> 56:31.320
Franco.

56:31.400 --> 56:33.240
Take my name out of his mouth.

56:35.400 --> 56:36.400
Ah!

56:37.160 --> 56:39.040
[groaning]

56:39.880 --> 56:41.480
[crunching]

56:43.240 --> 56:45.520
While you're there,
dig for gold.

56:45.600 --> 56:46.720
Pay for the petrol.

56:48.000 --> 56:49.000
[groans]

56:58.120 --> 56:59.680
[coughs]

57:01.960 --> 57:04.360
See how much I know about you?

57:04.440 --> 57:07.480
I even know what's in your
fucking mouth.

57:08.640 --> 57:10.240
Look at me. Look at me.

57:11.280 --> 57:12.520
Look at me.

57:14.120 --> 57:15.960
You take up with the Jews.

57:16.040 --> 57:18.560
You think that's what
London is all about.

57:18.640 --> 57:20.960
You can just come down,
pick a side.

57:21.040 --> 57:23.800
You fucking clown.

57:23.880 --> 57:26.400
Now your life is over.

57:26.480 --> 57:30.640
My face is the last thing
you will ever see on earth.

57:30.720 --> 57:34.520
Your mistake. You remember that
when you get to hell.

57:39.880 --> 57:41.320
Finish him off.

57:43.160 --> 57:44.640
[explosion]

57:44.720 --> 57:46.080
[indistinct yelling]

57:46.160 --> 57:47.600
[police whistle]

57:53.640 --> 57:55.000
[metal sounding cane]

58:09.040 --> 58:13.680
I suppose we should see
if the bastard's still alive.

58:15.080 --> 58:17.440
[dramatic music playing]

58:23.120 --> 58:25.360
[theme song playing]
